# Hackathon Rules (GlobeTrotter)

Follow these when building or reviewing any feature. Prefer choices that satisfy **Must have** over optional polish.

## Must have

- **Dynamic data**: Prefer APIs, DB queries, or other live/dynamic sources. Static JSON is OK only for early prototyping—replace before considering a feature done.
- **Responsive UI**: Clean layout, consistent color scheme and spacing; works on desktop and mobile.
- **Input validation**: Validate all user inputs (forms, search, dates, budgets). Fail clearly; never trust client-only checks for critical data.
- **Intuitive navigation**: Clear menus, logical placement, readable spacing. Users should reach trips, builder, budget, and search without hunting.
- **Git collaboration**: Meaningful commits and shared ownership. Do not treat a single-person “repo manager” workflow as enough for the team.

## Nice to have

- **Backend & DB**: Prefer real APIs, relational data modeling, and a local database over frontend-only mocks when building itineraries, stops, activities, and expenses.
- **No blind paste**: Understand and adapt AI/snippet code to this project before shipping it.
- **Offline / local**: Prefer solutions that still work without full cloud dependency when practical (local DB, cached data, graceful offline UX).
- **Purposeful stack**: Add libraries or trendy tech only when they clearly improve GlobeTrotter—not for novelty.

## Product alignment

Build toward the problem statement in `PROBLEM_STATEMENT.md`: multi-city itineraries, search, budgets, calendars/timelines, sharing, auth, and relational storage of trips/stops/activities/expenses.
